Rao\nDate: Mon Feb  9 02:19:51 2015\n\nDifference arrays are used in applications such as software testing, authentication codes and data compression. Pseudo-orthogonal Latin squares are used in experimental designs. A special class of pseudo-orthogonal Latin squares are the mutually nearly orthogonal Latin squares (MNOLS) first discussed in 2002, with general constructions given in 2007. In this paper we develop row complete MNOLS from difference covering arrays. We will use this connection to settle the spectrum question for sets of 3 mutually pseudo-orthogonal Latin squares of even order, for all but the order 146.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiﬀerence Covering Arrays and Pseudo- (https://avesis.yildiz.edu.tr/yayin/cd3f34fe-6f49-4961-bd6b-5989fee13239/difference-covering-arrays-and-pseudo-orthogonal-latin-squares/document.pdf)\nciteturn9search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Diﬀerence Covering Arrays and Pseudo- ... Pseudo-orthogonal Latin squares are used in experimental designs. ... In this paper we develop row complete MNOLS from diﬀerence covering\nDiﬀerence Covering Arrays and Pseudo-\nOrthogonal Latin Squares\nFatih Demirkale\nfatih.demirkale@uqconnect.edu.au\nDepartment of Mathematics,\nKo¸c University, Sarıyer, 34450, _Istanbul, Turkey\nDiane M. Donovan\ndmd@maths.uq.edu.au\nCentre for Discrete Mathematics and Computing,\nUniversity of Queensland, St Lucia 4072 Australia\nJoanne Hall\nj42.hall@qut.edu.au\nDepartment of Mathematics\nQueensland University of Technology\nQld, 4000\nj42.hall@qut.edu.au\nAbdollah Khodkar\nakhodkar@westga.edu\nDepartment of Mathematics\nUniversity of West Georgia\nCarrollton, GA 30118, USA\nAsha Rao\nasha@rmit.edu.au\nSchool of Mathematical and Geospacial Sciences\nRMIT University\narXiv:1502.02332v1  [math.CO]  9 Feb 2015\nVic 3000, Australia\nJanuary 19, 2015\nAbstract\nDiﬀerence arrays are used in applications such as software testing, authentication codes an